All three functions are written, and `python3 test_impl.py` now prints **ALL PASS**. I ran the tests after each function and left `test_impl.py` untouched.

- **`rle.py` `encode`**: groups each run of the same character and writes the character followed by its count, so `'aaabbc'` becomes `'a3b2c1'` and an empty string stays empty.
- **`rle.py` `decode`**: reads each character followed by its count (which can be more than one digit) and expands it.
- **`intervals.py` `merge`**: sorts the intervals by start, then joins any that overlap or touch at an endpoint, so `[1,2],[2,3]` becomes `[1,3]`.

The stress section of the tests took 2.8 seconds.

Limitation: the encoded format can't tell the string's own digits apart from the counts, so `decode(encode(s))` doesn't give back `s` if `s` contains digits. The tests and docstrings never use such input.
